                        "review": "I really enjoyed Blackbay Asylum, I’d almost call it a hidden gem, if it weren't for the absence of certain basic gameplay comfort features.\nIt’s an adventure and puzzle game, principally in a top-down view, but it also includes some first person sequences.\n\nThe story puts you in the shoes of Doug, a psychopathic murderer admitted to the Blackbay Asylum. Our dear Doug also happens to be a giant who wanders in diapers, and his best friend is a teddy bear named... Teddy. One day, his cell door is left open, and the adventure begins as you navigate the Asylum’s corridors, now littered with gruesomely mutilated corpses. Expect mad scientist, crazed mutated patients, and an ancient evil from a forgotten civilization.\nSo, it's a horror game that, in the end, offers a fairly interesting story. But it's also a quite comical game thanks to Doug's numerous comments, which are tinged with dark humor, irony, or occasionally break the 4th wall. Be warned, however, some visuals can be quite repulsive or disturbing. \n\nThe gameplay is the classic: find items, solve puzzles. I enjoyed it, everything feels fairly logical, and there are plenty of puzzles to solve. Some include clues hidden in text (poems, suicide notes, diary entries, or cultist writings) so a decent grasp of English is necessary. Certain puzzles may require you to take notes, though nothing is too complicated. The real challenge lies in spotting the items or interactive elements, as the game's readability is sometimes lacking.\nThat said, the game is packed with amusing little remarks triggered by interacting with the environment, making the act of scouring the Asylum an enjoyable experience. You can interact with almost everything, from dismembered corpses to trash cans and posters on the walls, and each interaction is accompanied by an amusing comment from Doug.\n\nA few downsides that could have easily been avoided:\nEvery time you launch the game, you have to redefine the display resolution.\nThe WASD controls cannot be remapped, though the arrow keys are available as an alternative.\nThe game does not allow saving in the middle of a chapter. The save is made at the end of each chapter. Blackbay Asylum is divided into 10 chapters, ranging from 5 to 45 min, and the longer ones include temporary checkpoints.\nIt's easy to miss things, be it an item or interaction. I sometimes had to cheat a little by using a guide, only to realize I had simply missed an area (ex: a staircase hidden behind another staircase). Some visual sign, or a dedicated key to highlight possible interactions, would have been very useful.\n\nUltimately, the strengths of Blackbay Asylum far outweigh its flaws, and this game was, for me, a very pleasant surprise.",
